<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Turin</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Torino, Augusta Taurinorum</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Turin is a city in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> on the Po river and the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a>. Founded as the Roman colony of Augusta Taurinorum, it became the capital of the dukes of Savoy in 1563 and later of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a>. It led the unification of Italy and was the first capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy</a> from 1861 to 1865, before growing into a major industrial city as the home of FIAT.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/turin.jpg" alt="Turin" width="320" height="161" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Zairon · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Torino_Piazza_Castello_11.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Turin lies in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, mainly on the western bank of the River Po, surrounded on the west and north by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> and on the east by a high hill that continues the hills of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montferrat">Monferrato</a>. Four major rivers pass through it: the Po and its tributaries the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dora-riparia">Dora Riparia</a>, the Stura di Lanzo and the Sangone.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Taurini, a Celto-Ligurian Alpine people, occupied the upper valley of the Po. In 218 BCE Hannibal, allied with their enemies the Insubres, took their chief town, Taurasia, after a three-day siege. In the 1st century BCE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> founded Augusta Taurinorum, a town of about 5,000 inhabitants within high walls; its Porta Palatina is still preserved. After the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-roman-empire">Western Roman Empire</a> the town was held by the Heruli, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ostrogoths">Ostrogoths</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombards</a>, and passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> in 773. The Counts of Savoy gained control after the marriage of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adelaide-of-susa">Adelaide of Susa</a> to Otto, son of Humbert Biancamano, and when the city was annexed to the Duchy of Savoy at the end of the 13th century it had 20,000 inhabitants.</p><p>Emmanuel Philibert, nicknamed Iron Head, made Turin the capital of the Duchy of Savoy in 1563. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-colonial-empire">French Empire</a> annexed it with the rest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a> in 1802, and in 1814 the Kingdom of Piedmont-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> was restored with Turin as its capital. That kingdom led the struggle for the unification of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, and Turin was the political and intellectual centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-unification">Risorgimento</a>. It became the capital of the new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy</a> in 1861; the capital moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> in 1865 and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> after the conquest of the Papal States in 1870.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Turin is the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a> and is sometimes called the cradle of Italian liberty. Its architecture is predominantly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a>, and part of the historical centre is inscribed in the World Heritage List as the Residences of the Royal House of Savoy. It is the base of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fiat">Fiat</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lancia">Lancia</a> and Alfa Romeo, and hosted the 2006 Winter Olympics.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Turin” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Two years later the extinction of the Savoy-Achaea branch brought the principality of Piedmont, with <b>Turin</b>, back under the main line.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-savoy">Duchy of Savoy · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Its driving force was the Kingdom of Sardinia, ruled from <b>Turin</b> by the House of Savoy and guided by its prime minister, Camillo Benso, Count of Cavour.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Under the House of Savoy the island remained distinct from the much richer mainland of Piedmont, with its silk industry, wine and the growing city of <b>Turin</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-sardinia">Kingdom of Sardinia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Congress of Vienna made Monaco a protectorate of Sardinia in 1815; Sardinian forces withdrew under the Treaty of <b>Turin</b> in 1860 and Monaco became a French protectorate once more.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-monaco">Principality of Monaco · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Codex Ambrosianus in Milan and the Codex Taurinensis in <b>Turin</b>, 193 leaves in five parts, hold passages from the gospels, the Epistles and Nehemiah.</blockquote><a href="/language/gothic">Gothic · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote>Copies are held in many museums, among them the British Museum, the Museo Egizio in <b>Turin</b>, the Rijksmuseum van Oudheden in Leiden and the Royal Ontario Museum; the Joseph Smith Papyri survive only as fragments, many destroyed in modern times.</blockquote><a href="/work/book-of-the-dead">Book of the Dead · Survival &amp; Rediscovery</a></li></ul></section>
